VALUES ('c195ab35496d0e8d64bebb827c19bbe0d0cb950457cc52e5751e8739c3cb48ef', 'Biology', 1, 'Science', 'Admissions test', 'ESAT', $t48${"topics":[{"title":"B1. Cells","subtopics":["Know and understand the structure and function of the main sub-cellular components of eukaryotic cells (both animal and plant), including cell membrane, cytoplasm, nucleus, mitochondrion, cell wall, chloroplast and vacuole (plant only).","Know and understand the structure and function of the main sub-cellular components of prokaryotic cells (bacteria), including cell membrane, cytoplasm, cell wall, chromosomal DNA/no 'true' nucleus, and plasmid DNA.","Know and understand the levels of organisation within organisms as: cells to tissues to organs to organ systems."]},{"title":"B2. Movement across membranes","subtopics":["Know and understand the processes of diffusion, osmosis (in terms of water potential), and active transport, including examples in living and non-living systems."]},{"title":"B3. Cell division and sex determination","subtopics":["Mitosis and the cell cycle: know and understand that the mitotic cell cycle includes interphase and mitosis producing two genetically identical daughter cells; understand the importance of mitosis in growth, repair, replacement and asexual reproduction; know that cancer results from mutations leading to uncontrolled cell division.","Meiosis and the cell cycle: know and understand that the meiotic cell cycle includes interphase and meiosis producing four genetically different haploid daughter cells; know and understand the role of meiosis in producing genetically different gametes so the zygote is diploid.","Asexual and sexual reproduction: know and understand that asexual reproduction involves one parent and genetically identical offspring, while sexual reproduction involves two parents and genetically different offspring, leading to variation.","Sex determination: know that in most mammals including humans, females are XX and males are XY; analyse genetic data and diagrams to establish the sex and ratio of offspring."]},{"title":"B4. Inheritance","subtopics":["Know and understand the nucleus as a site of genetic material in eukaryotic cells.","Know and understand the following genetic terms: gene, allele, dominant, recessive, heterozygous, homozygous, phenotype, genotype, chromosome, autosome.","Monohybrid crosses: use and interpret genetic data and diagrams involving monohybrid (single gene) crosses; use and interpret family trees/pedigrees and express outcomes as ratios, numbers, probabilities or percentages; understand inherited conditions and that most phenotypes result from multiple genes."]},{"title":"B5. DNA","subtopics":["Know and understand that the genome is the full set of genetic material (DNA) of an organism, and that this DNA is contained within chromosomes.","Know and understand the structure of DNA: single-stranded (ssDNA) and double-stranded (dsDNA) polymers of nucleotides forming a double helix; know the structure of a nucleotide (sugar, phosphate, nitrogenous base).","Protein synthesis: know and understand that protein synthesis produces polypeptide chains of amino acids, that the 3D shape of a protein is determined by its amino acid sequence, and that the nucleotide sequence in a gene (read as triplets) determines the amino acid sequence of the polypeptide.","Gene mutations: understand that a mutation changes the sequence of nucleotides in DNA, and know that most mutations have no effect on the phenotype, some have a small effect, and occasionally others determine the phenotype."]},{"title":"B6. Gene technologies","subtopics":["Genetic engineering: understand the process of genetic engineering, including taking a copy of a gene from one organism, inserting it into the DNA of another, and the roles of restriction enzymes and ligases; recall examples and explain the benefits and risks of genetic engineering in medical applications.","Stem cells: know and understand that early embryonic cells are totipotent, most embryonic stem cells are pluripotent, and adult stem cells are multipotent; know and understand the likely benefits and risks of using stem cells in medical applications.","Selective breeding: know and understand the differences and similarities between natural selection and selective breeding, and understand the impact of selective breeding on populations."]},{"title":"B7. Variation","subtopics":["Natural selection and evolution: know that there is usually extensive genetic variation within a population of a species; describe evolution as a change in inherited characteristics over time through natural selection, potentially forming a new species; know that antibiotic resistance in bacteria is an example of evolution through natural selection.","Sources of variation: understand that variation can be genetic/inherited or environmental, both affecting a range of phenotypes."]},{"title":"B8. Enzymes","subtopics":["Know and understand that enzymes are primarily proteins that function as biological catalysts.","Know and understand the general mechanism of enzyme action, including the role of the active site and enzyme specificity.","Know and understand how the factors of temperature and pH can affect the rate of enzyme action.","Know the role of amylases, proteases and lipases in the digestion of carbohydrates, proteins and fats."]},{"title":"B9. Animal physiology","subtopics":["Respiration: know and understand the processes of cellular respiration, aerobic respiration (including word equation), and anaerobic respiration in animal cells (including word equation).","Organ systems: know and understand the structure and function of the nervous system (CNS, neurones, synapses, reflex arc), respiratory system (structure, ventilation, gas exchange, surface area:volume ratio), circulatory system (heart, heart rate, ECGs, blood vessels, blood composition, exchange surfaces), and digestive system (structure, peristalsis, digestion, absorption, egestion).","Excretory system and homeostasis: know and understand the structure and function of the excretory system including the kidney and nephron and its role in homeostasis; understand negative feedback, the regulation of blood glucose (insulin and glucagon), type 1 and type 2 diabetes, and the regulation of water content (including ADH) and body temperature.","Hormones: know and understand that hormones are released from endocrine glands and travel via the blood to target structures; know the main role of adrenaline; know the roles of hormones in human reproduction, including the menstrual cycle (FSH, LH, oestrogen, progesterone) and hormonal/non-hormonal contraception.","Disease and body defence: know that communicable diseases are caused by pathogenic bacteria, viruses, protists and fungi, including STIs and HIV/AIDS; understand treatment of disease including antibiotics and vaccines, and the process of drug/vaccine discovery and testing; know that non-communicable diseases (cardiovascular disease, cancers, diabetes) are caused by interacting factors and can be treated/managed by medication, surgery and lifestyle changes."]},{"title":"B10. Ecosystems","subtopics":["Levels of organisation in an ecosystem: know and understand organisation from individuals to populations to communities to ecosystems; understand abiotic/biotic factors, population change, interdependence (predation, mutualism, parasitism), competition, and the role of photosynthetic organisms as primary producers of biomass.","Material cycling: know and understand the carbon cycle (photosynthesis, respiration, combustion, decomposition) and the importance of the water cycle to living organisms.","Biodiversity: know and understand how quadrats and belt transects are used to investigate distribution and abundance of organisms and determine numbers in a given area; know and understand positive and negative human interactions in an ecosystem, including fish farming, acid rain and eutrophication, and their impact on biodiversity."]},{"title":"B11. Plant physiology","subtopics":["Importance of photosynthesis: know and understand photosynthesis as an endothermic reaction using light energy to react carbon dioxide and water to produce glucose and oxygen; understand the effect of temperature, light intensity and carbon dioxide concentration as limiting factors on rate.","Transport systems in plants: know and understand how xylem and phloem structures are adapted to their functions; know how water and mineral ions are taken up by plants (root hair cells); know the processes of transpiration and translocation, including stomata structure/function, effect of environmental factors, and the rate of transpiration calculation."]}],"coverage":{"verdict":"grounded","topics_grounded":11,"topics_total":11,"source_url":"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/03165424/ESAT_Content_Specification.pdf"}}$t48$::jsonb, $c49${"verdict":"grounded","topics_grounded":11,"topics_total":11,"source_url":"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/03165424/ESAT_Content_Specification.pdf"}$c49$::jsonb, 'claude-sonnet-5 (manual spec extraction, human-verified, not AI-generated)')
