VALUES ('c195ab35496d0e8d64bebb827c19bbe0d0cb950457cc52e5751e8739c3cb48ef', 'Chemistry', 1, 'Science', 'Admissions test', 'ESAT', $t50${"topics":[{"title":"C1. Atomic structure","subtopics":["Describe the structure of the atom as a central nucleus (containing protons and neutrons) surrounded by electrons moving in shells/energy levels.","Know the relative masses and charges of protons, neutrons and electrons, and recognise that most of the mass of an atom is in the nucleus.","Know and be able to use the terms atomic number and mass number, together with standard notation, to calculate the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in any atom or ion.","Use the atomic number to write the electron configurations of the first 20 elements in the Periodic Table (H to Ca) in comma-separated format.","Know the definition of isotopes as atoms of an element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons; use data, including from a mass spectrometer, to identify the number and abundances of different isotopes of elements.","Know and use the concept of relative atomic mass, Ar, including calculating values from given data."]},{"title":"C2. The Periodic Table","subtopics":["Know that Periods are horizontal rows and Groups are vertical columns.","Know that the elements are arranged in the order of increasing atomic number.","Recall the position of metals and non-metals in the Periodic Table: alkali metals (Group 1), alkaline earth metals (Group 2), common non-metals in Group 16, the halogens (Group 17), the noble gases (Group 18) and the transition metals.","Know and use the relationship between the position of an atom in the Periodic Table (Group and Period) and the electron configuration of the atom.","Understand that elements in the same Group have similar chemical properties, and that down a metal Group reactivity increases while down a non-metal Group reactivity decreases."]},{"title":"C3. Chemical reactions, formulae and equations","subtopics":["Understand that in a chemical reaction, new substances are formed by the rearrangement of atoms and their electrons, but no nuclei are destroyed or created.","Know the chemical formulae of simple, common ionic and covalent compounds.","Know and use state symbols: solid (s), liquid (l), gas (g), aqueous solution (aq).","Be able to construct and balance a chemical equation, including ionic and half-equations.","Understand that chemical reactions can be reversible and reach a state of equilibrium in a closed system; know the factors that affect the position of an equilibrium (concentration, temperature, overall pressure) and predict the effect of changing them."]},{"title":"C4. Quantitative chemistry","subtopics":["Use Ar values to calculate the relative molar mass, Mr.","Know that Avogadro's number gives the number of particles in one mole of a substance.","Know that one mole of a substance is the Ar or Mr in grams, and perform conversions of grams to moles and vice versa.","Calculate the percentage composition by mass of a compound using given Ar values.","Know that the empirical formula is the simplest integer ratio of atoms in a compound; find the empirical formula from percentage composition or reacting masses, and the molecular formula given the Mr value.","Use balanced chemical equations to calculate the masses of reactants and products, including where there is a limiting reactant.","Be able to construct balanced chemical equations from reacting masses or gas volumes data.","Understand that (for an ideal gas) one mole of a gas occupies a set volume at a given temperature and pressure, and perform conversions of volumes to number of moles and vice versa.","Solutions: understand that concentration can be measured in mol dm⁻³ or g dm⁻³, and calculate concentration given moles/mass and volume; know the term saturated solution and calculate/interpret solubility data.","Use the concentrations of solutions and the reacting ratio of reactants from the balanced equation to perform titration calculations.","Calculate the percentage yield of a reaction using the balanced chemical equation and the percentage yield formula."]},{"title":"C5. Oxidation, reduction and redox","subtopics":["Know that on a basic level, oxidation is the gain of oxygen and reduction is the removal of oxygen.","Know and be able to use the concept that oxidation and reduction are the transfer of electrons: reduction is the gain of electrons and oxidation is the loss of electrons.","Determine and use the oxidation states of atoms in simple inorganic compounds.","Identify any chemical equation that involves oxidation only, reduction only, redox, or no oxidation/reduction.","Understand the concept of disproportionation and recognise reactions (or species) where this occurs.","Understand the terms oxidising agent and reducing agent, and be able to identify them in reactions."]},{"title":"C6. Chemical bonding, structure and properties","subtopics":["Define and understand the differences between elements, compounds and mixtures.","Understand that atoms often react to form compounds which have the electron configuration of a noble gas (Group 18); understand that the type of bonding depends on the atoms involved.","Ionic bonding: know that ions are formed by transfer of electrons from metals to non-metals; predict the charge of the most stable ions formed from elements in Groups 1, 2, 16, 17 and aluminium; know the chemical formulae of common compound ions and how Roman numerals denote oxidation states; determine formulae of ionic compounds; understand the general physical properties of ionic compounds.","Covalent bonding: know that a covalent bond is formed when atoms share one or more pairs of electrons; understand that covalently bonded substances can be small molecules or giant structures; understand the general physical properties of each.","Metallic bonding: understand that solid metals exist as a giant structure of positively charged ions surrounded by delocalised (free) electrons; understand the general physical properties of metals.","Understand that intermolecular forces can exist between molecules, and that these forces must be overcome in melting and boiling.","Be able to relate structure and bonding to physical properties, such as melting point and conductivity."]},{"title":"C7. Group chemistry","subtopics":["Know the physical and chemical properties of the alkali metals (Group 1), the halogens (Group 17) and the noble gases (Group 18).","Describe the trends in chemical reactivity and physical properties of the alkali metals (Group 1) and make predictions based on those trends, including the relative positions of lithium, sodium and potassium.","The halogens (Group 17): describe the trends in chemical reactivity and physical properties, including relative positions of fluorine, chlorine, bromine and iodine; explain displacement reactions in terms of reactivity competition between halogens and halide ions."]},{"title":"C8. Separation techniques","subtopics":["Know that chemical processes are required to displace constituent elements from their compounds.","Know that physical processes are required to separate mixtures, including miscible/immiscible liquids and dissolved/insoluble solids.","Know when to apply the following separation techniques: simple/fractional distillation, paper chromatography (including Rf values), separating funnel, centrifugation, dissolving, filtration, evaporation and crystallisation.","Know how to establish the purity of a substance using chromatography."]},{"title":"C9. Acids, bases and salts","subtopics":["Acids: define an acid as a substance that can form H⁺(aq) ions or is an H⁺ donor; describe reactions with metals, carbonates, metal hydroxides and metal oxides forming salts; understand strong, weak, dilute and concentrated; know that oxides of non-metals can form acidic solutions; recall that pH is a measure of H⁺ ion concentration and that a change of 1 on the pH scale corresponds to a factor of 10 change in concentration; know the terms mono-, di-, tri- and polyprotic.","Bases: define a base as a substance that can form OH⁻(aq) ions or is an H⁺ acceptor; understand strong, weak, dilute and concentrated; know that some metal oxides and hydroxides react with water to form alkaline solutions.","Know that the reaction of an acid with a base can lead to neutralisation and is often exothermic."]},{"title":"C10. Rates of reaction","subtopics":["Describe the qualitative effects on rate of reaction of concentration, temperature, particle size, a catalyst and, for gases, pressure.","Know that the rate of reaction can be found by measuring the loss of a reactant or gain of a product, or by measurement of a physical property over time.","Interpret data in graphical form concerning the rate of a reaction.","Use collision theory to explain changes in the rate of a reaction.","Understand that particles must have sufficient energy when they collide to react, and that this energy is called the activation energy (Ea); identify the activation energy on an energy level diagram.","Know that catalysts are not used up in a reaction, are chemically unchanged at the end, provide an alternative route (reaction mechanism) with lower activation energy, and do not affect the position of an equilibrium."]},{"title":"C11. Energetics","subtopics":["Understand the concepts of an exothermic reaction (ΔH negative) and an endothermic reaction (ΔH positive).","Know that if a reversible reaction is exothermic in one direction, it is endothermic in the other direction.","Be able to interpret energy level diagrams.","Be able to calculate energy changes from specific heat capacities and changes in temperature in calorimetry experiments.","Know that bond breaking is endothermic and bond formation is exothermic, and be able to use bond energy data to calculate energy changes."]},{"title":"C12. Electrolysis","subtopics":["Understand the terms electrode, cathode (negative electrode), anode (positive electrode) and electrolyte.","Understand why direct current (dc), and not alternating current (ac), is used in electrolysis.","Understand that in electrolysis at the cathode, cations receive electrons (reduction), and at the anode, anions lose electrons (oxidation).","Understand and be able to predict the products of the electrolysis of aqueous solutions (including those of salts) and molten binary compounds.","Be able to write half-equations for the processes taking place at each electrode.","Explain how electrolysis is used to electroplate objects."]},{"title":"C13. Carbon/Organic chemistry","subtopics":["General concepts: know that crude oil is the main source of hydrocarbons, separated by fractional distillation; understand the link between carbon chain length and boiling point/viscosity/flammability; know the use of cracking to form shorter chain alkanes and alkenes; understand structural isomerism; understand molecular/structural formula terms; understand complete/incomplete combustion; know IUPAC naming guidelines; know the terms homologous series and functional group.","Alkanes (saturated hydrocarbons): describe alkanes as a homologous series with general formula CnH2n+2; be able to name or recognise the C1 to C6 straight-chain alkanes.","Alkenes (unsaturated hydrocarbons): describe alkenes as a homologous series with a double bond and general formula CnH2n; name/recognise C2 to C6 straight-chain alkenes including position of the double bond; recognise the bromine water test for unsaturation; know addition reactions with hydrogen, halogens, hydrogen halides and steam.","Polymers: addition polymerisation of alkenes/polyalkenes (recognising monomer/polymer structure and repeating unit); condensation polymerisation forming polyesters and polyamides (including amino acids forming proteins); understand the terms biodegradable and non-biodegradable.","Alcohols: describe alcohols as a homologous series with general formula CnH2n+1OH; name/recognise C1 to C6 straight-chain alcohols; describe the reaction of alcohols with sodium metal.","Carboxylic acids: describe carboxylic acids as a homologous series with general formula CnH2n+1COOH; name/recognise C1 to C6 straight-chain carboxylic acids; describe their chemical properties as weak acids and predict reactions/salt formulae; know that carboxylic acids react with alcohols in the presence of an acid catalyst to produce esters."]},{"title":"C14. Metals","subtopics":["Understand that the reactivity of a metal is linked to its tendency to form positive ions and the ease of extraction of the metal.","Be able to use displacement reactions to establish the order of reactivity of metals and vice versa.","Describe how the uses of metals are related to their physical and chemical properties (e.g. Al, Fe, Cu, Ag, Au, Ti), and understand that alloys can be formed to produce materials with specific properties.","Know that most metal ores are the oxides of the metal, and that the extraction of metals always involves reduction processes.","Know that common properties of transition metals include: forming stable ions in different oxidation states, often forming coloured compounds, and often being used as catalysts (as ions or atoms)."]},{"title":"C15. Kinetic/Particle theory","subtopics":["Be able to describe the packing and movement of particles in the three states of matter: solid, liquid and gas.","Understand the changes to the packing and movement of particles in changes of state (freezing, melting, boiling/evaporating, condensing), including the energy required related to bonding/structure and intermolecular forces."]},{"title":"C16. Chemical tests","subtopics":["Know and recognise the tests for gases: hydrogen (squeaky pop), oxygen (relights a glowing splint), carbon dioxide (limewater turns cloudy), chlorine (bleaches damp blue litmus paper).","Know, recognise and describe the tests for anions: carbonates (dilute acid), halides (silver nitrate/dilute nitric acid), sulfates (barium chloride/dilute hydrochloric acid).","Know and recognise the test for metal cations using aqueous sodium hydroxide: Al³⁺/Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ (white precipitate), Cu²⁺ (blue precipitate), Fe²⁺ (green precipitate), Fe³⁺ (brown precipitate).","Recall and recognise the flame test for the cations of Li (crimson red), Na (yellow-orange), K (lilac), Ca (red-orange), Cu (green).","Know and recognise the test for the presence of water using anhydrous copper(II) sulfate (colour change from white to blue)."]},{"title":"C17. Air and water","subtopics":["Know and be able to use the composition of dry air, and understand that fractional distillation can be used to separate the components of air.","Know the origins and describe the effects of greenhouse gases such as CO2 and CH4.","Know the origins and effects of gaseous pollutants such as CO, CO2, SO2 and NOx.","Know the purpose of chlorine and fluoride ions in the treatment of drinking water."]}],"coverage":{"verdict":"grounded","topics_grounded":17,"topics_total":17,"source_url":"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/03165424/ESAT_Content_Specification.pdf"}}$t50$::jsonb, $c51${"verdict":"grounded","topics_grounded":17,"topics_total":17,"source_url":"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/03165424/ESAT_Content_Specification.pdf"}$c51$::jsonb, 'claude-sonnet-5 (manual spec extraction, human-verified, not AI-generated)')
